  <p>Tracking only helps if it changes what you do. Jordan Hoppel's rule is to track what you can act on.</p>

  <h2>What to track</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sets and reps</li>
    <li>Rest time</li>
    <li>Tempo</li>
    <li>The lifts used</li>
    <li>How you felt that day</li>
    <li>Anything that gives context on the session</li>
  </ul>

  <JordanQuote>If it's not in your control it's probably not worth tracking. It will cause more harm than good.</JordanQuote>

  <h2>How to tell you're getting stronger</h2>
  <p>If the weights you're moving continually go up according to the schedule and progression you should be following, every single week, you're getting stronger.</p>

  <Media type="image" alt="Workout log showing weight increasing week over week" caption="Rising weights, week over week." brief="Screenshot: client log from the training app (get client permission)." />

  <h2>Spreadsheet vs. app</h2>
  <p>A spreadsheet only wins if you don't want technology around and don't want to be distracted by a phone. On data, organization, and capabilities, an app always wins because it has built-in calculations that go far beyond a clunky spreadsheet.</p>

  <h2>What makes a good lifting app</h2>
  <p>Jordan wants the programs and intelligence to come from a coach with experience, not a large language model. In his view LLMs have no skin in the game and miss information a coach picks up on. AI is fine for menial, time-consuming tasks and organization.</p>
  <p>What annoys him: most legacy lifting apps are like BlackBerry compared to Apple. They're clunky, with legacy settings that aren't customizable, so each person doesn't get a good experience.</p>
